# `.grem/config.yaml`

`.grem/config.yaml` is the stamped project's grem control record. It is
separate from the modular project knowledge under `doc/`.

Agents ignore `.grem/**` during ordinary work. The control record and harness
become active only when the user explicitly requests a grem workflow or
supplies a grem prompt for execution.

## Shape

```yaml
kind: layout
schema: 1
template: python
template_version: 0.15.0
grem_version: 0.2.0
variables:
  project_name: myproject
  package_name: myproject
  proposal_prefix: MYPROJECT
generated:
  directories: []
  files: []
agent_instructions:
  central: .grem/harness/README.md
  targets:
    - AGENTS.md
    - CLAUDE.md
```

`template` and `template_version` identify the stamped template, and
`grem_version` records the grem CLI version that stamped or last upgraded the
project. `generated` records the paths supplied by that template.
`agent_instructions` selects the central instruction source and the
tool-specific files that an LLM agent should keep aligned.

`variables` holds the project-owned template parameters resolved at `init`:
`project_name` (from `--name` or the target folder), the derived `package_name`
(the `src/<package_name>` slug) and `proposal_prefix` (the uppercased proposal
ID prefix). Template content references them as `{{ project_name }}` tokens and
the scaffolder substitutes them (see [[templates]]). They are project-owned, so
`grem upgrade` reads them back to re-render deterministically and never
overwrites them — edit these values to rename or repurpose the project.

The config contains no timestamps or machine-specific paths. Template
`bootstrap.py` metadata and the template copy of `.grem/config.yaml` must agree.

The [[grem-cli]] reads this file for `diff`, `sync`, `agent`, `new`, and
`upgrade`, and `init` writes it (stamping `grem_version`).
